package agentsdoc

import "strings"

// writeV04dSection documents what 0.4d adds for an authoring agent, as its
// phases land. This one is rich text: `<text runs="path"/>` with the
// generated `span`, and `wrap: pre-wrap`. It names no path of the
// repository, since the project briefing (ProjectMarkdown) is this same
// text.
func writeV04dSection(b *strings.Builder) {
	b.WriteString("### v0.4d: rich text runs and `pre-wrap`\n\n")
	b.WriteString("- `<text id=\"reply\" runs=\"msg.runs\"/>` paints the runs at `msg.runs`: pieces of text that the host built (from markdown, say), each with its own classes. The document only styles them. `runs` is a path, resolved every frame as `bind` is (through the `each` and `with` aliases in scope, then the store); `runs=\"{r}\"` is `V003` (`runs=\"{r}\" is not a path`), and so is a `{$name}` in a template's body. The attribute is current-language only: in a document that writes `version` it is `V002` (`attribute \"runs\" on <text> (requires a <tui> without version)`). A `<text runs>` takes no body text: character data other than whitespace is `V013` (`<text runs> takes no body text: its text comes from the runs`) and is ignored.\n")
	b.WriteString("- The value at the path is an array of runs, a string (one run), or `null` (no run, no diagnostic). A run is a string, or an object with a string `text` and an optional `class`, a string of class names separated by spaces (a `class` of `null` is none); other members are ignored. A missing path is `B003` (`runs path \"r\" is missing`). A value of the wrong shape is `B013`, a warning, once per `<text>` and frame, for the first bad run: `runs=\"r\" holds a number, not an array of runs, a string, or null` (the text is empty), `runs=\"r\": run 1 is a number, not a string or an object` and `runs=\"r\": run 2 has no string \"text\"` (the run is left out), and `runs=\"r\": run 3 has a \"class\" that is not a list of class names` (the run is painted without classes). `validate` without `--data` checks none of them.\n")
	b.WriteString("- The text of a run is literal data: no `{…}` in it is read. It is normalized in this order: escape sequences are removed whole, each TAB becomes four spaces, and every other control but LF is removed, so `\"a\\u001b\\tb\"` is `a    b` and `\"a\\u001b(Bb\"` is `ab`. An LF is a hard line break. A `{path}` in a body keeps its older rule, which removes a TAB.\n")
	b.WriteString("- Each run is styled as a generated node of type `span`, a child of its `text`, with the run's classes. A span is not a box: it is not in the dump's `nodes`, has no id, and cannot be a `to=`. Select it by type and class, alone or as the child of its text: `span`, `.code`, `span.code`, `text > span`, `#reply > span.code`, `text.h > span`. It has no state, so no pseudo-class matches it itself (`#reply:focus > span` checks `:focus` on the text). It styles its cells with `color`, `background`, `bold`, `dim`, `italic`, `underline`, and `reverse`; any property that no rule sets on it is its text's, `background` included, and any other property is accepted and ignored. Its colors resolve `$token`s under the frame's theme. A written `<span>` is `V001` in every language (`unknown tag <span>; spans are generated from <text runs=\"…\">`; a document that writes `version` keeps `unknown tag <span>; use <text>`), and a type selector naming `span` in a document that writes `version` is `V003` (`selector \"S\": type \"span\" (requires a <tui> without version)`).\n")
	b.WriteString("- The joined text of the runs is the text: measuring, `wrap`, the `truncate*` values, `pre-wrap`, and `content-align` act on it as on a body, so a word may cross runs (`**foo**bar` is one word in two styles). A cluster takes the style of the run of its first code point; the joining space of `wrap: wrap` takes the style of the first space it stands for; the ellipsis and the rest of the content box take the text's own style. The dump's `text` is the joined text before wrapping, and `--styles` shows the runs cell by cell. `tuimark inspect FILE --at X,Y` on a cell a run painted adds `run`, `{\"index\", \"text\", \"classes\", \"style\"}`, the `style` entries of the seven properties of a span; a cell after the text or on an ellipsis has none, and neither has `--id`. `link` and runs in a table cell are not part of 0.4d.\n")
	b.WriteString("- `wrap: pre-wrap` (current language; in a document that writes `version` it is `V003`, `wrap: value \"pre-wrap\" (want …) (requires a <tui> without version)`) keeps every space and line break of a text and wraps it at the content width, as CSS's `white-space: pre-wrap` does, for code and diffs. Each hard line is split into segments, spaces and words. A segment of spaces always joins the line, and the spaces past the width hang and are not painted; a word joins the line when it fits, else it starts the next line, so a line may hold only spaces; a word wider than the width is cut at cluster boundaries. `PreWrap(\"    if x:\", 10)` is one line, `PreWrap(\"return  [1,   2,\", 10)` is `return  ` and `[1,   2,`, `PreWrap(\"a\\n\", 10)` is `a` and an empty line, and `PreWrap(\"\", 10)` is no line: an empty text is 0 lines under every `wrap`. Its intrinsic width is the widest hard line, as for `nowrap`. It never reports `L008` for cut columns, and reports it for lines that do not fit unless `overflow: hidden` is written. With `content-align`, a line is aligned without its trailing spaces. A table cell, a `checkbox`, and a `select` paint `pre-wrap` as `truncate`.\n\n")
}
